<body>
    <div class="container">
        <header>
            <h1>🏥 The Need for Hepatitis C Treatment</h1>
            <p>Understanding the global burden and the importance of early intervention</p>
        </header>

        <div class="content">
            <!-- Global Impact Section -->
            <section>
                <h2>Global Impact of Hepatitis C</h2>
                <p>Hepatitis C virus (HCV) remains a significant public health challenge worldwide. Millions of people are living with chronic hepatitis C, unaware of their infection status or the availability of highly effective treatments. The World Health Organization estimates that approximately <strong>130-150 million people</strong> are chronically infected with hepatitis C globally.</p>

                <div class="stats-grid">
                    <div class="stat-card">
                        <div class="number">130-150M</div>
                        <div class="label">People with chronic HCV infection worldwide</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-card">
                        <div class="number">3-4M</div>
                        <div class="label">New infections annually</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-card">
                        <div class="number">399K</div>
                        <div class="label">Deaths annually</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-card">
                        <div class="number">95%+</div>
                        <div class="label">Cure rate with treatment</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Why Treatment is Critical -->
            <section>
                <h2>Why Treatment is Critical</h2>

                <h3>Preventing Disease Progression</h3>
                <p>Without treatment, chronic hepatitis C can lead to serious liver complications. Over 15-30 years, untreated infection can progress to:</p>
                <div class="highlights">
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Fibrosis:</strong> Scarring of the liver tissue</li>
                        <li><strong>Cirrhosis:</strong> Advanced liver damage reducing function</li>
                        <li><strong>Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC):</strong> Liver cancer</li>
                        <li><strong>Liver Failure:</strong> Life-threatening complications</li>
                        <li><strong>Death:</strong> Without intervention</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3>Reducing Transmission</h3>
                <p>Treating hepatitis C not only saves individual lives but also helps prevent transmission to others. An undetectable viral load means an untransmittable condition, helping to break the chain of infection.</p>

                <h3>Improving Quality of Life</h3>
                <p>Chronic hepatitis C can cause fatigue, joint pain, depression, and other debilitating symptoms. Treatment can significantly improve quality of life and overall well-being.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- Treatment Breakthroughs -->
            <section>
                <h2>Modern Treatment Breakthroughs</h2>
                <p>The landscape of hepatitis C treatment has transformed dramatically in recent years. Direct-Acting Antivirals (DAAs) have revolutionized the management of hepatitis C:</p>

                <div class="treatment-benefits">
                    <h3>✓ Benefits of Modern Treatment</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>High Cure Rates:</strong> Over 95% of patients achieve sustained virological response (SVR)</li>
                        <li><strong>Shorter Duration:</strong> Most treatments last 8-12 weeks</li>
                        <li><strong>Minimal Side Effects:</strong> DAAs are generally well-tolerated with few adverse effects</li>
                        <li><strong>Oral Administration:</strong> Easy-to-take pills, no need for injections</li>
                        <li><strong>All Genotypes:</strong> Treatment options available for all hepatitis C genotypes</li>
                        <li><strong>Pan-Genotypic Agents:</strong> Single regimens effective against multiple HCV variants</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3>Treatment Timeline</h3>
                <p><strong>Week 1-4:</strong> Viral load begins to decrease significantly<br>
                <strong>Week 8-12:</strong> Most patients achieve undetectable viral load<br>
                <strong>Week 12+:</strong> Sustained Virological Response (SVR) confirmed</p>
            </section>

            <!-- Barriers and Solutions -->
            <section>
                <h2>Addressing Barriers to Treatment</h2>

                <h3>Common Barriers</h3>
                <div class="highlights">
                    <ul>
                        <li>Cost and access to treatment</li>
                        <li>Lack of awareness and diagnosis</li>
                        <li>Stigma associated with HCV infection</li>
                        <li>Limited healthcare infrastructure in some regions</li>
                        <li>Drug interactions and comorbidities</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3>Potential Solutions</h3>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-position: inside;">
                    <li><strong>Generics:</strong> Increasing availability of generic DAAs reduces treatment costs</li>
                    <li><strong>Public Health Programs:</strong> Screening and treatment initiatives in resource-limited settings</li>
                    <li><strong>Education:</strong> Raising awareness about symptoms, testing, and treatment</li>
                    <li><strong>Telemedicine:</strong> Remote consultations expanding access to specialists</li>
                    <li><strong>Elimination Programs:</strong> WHO targets for hepatitis C elimination by 2030</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- Testing and Diagnosis -->
            <section>
                <h2>Testing and Early Diagnosis</h2>

                <div class="warning-box">
                    <strong>⚠️ Important:</strong> Many people with hepatitis C don't know they have it. If you have risk factors or have never been tested, speak with your healthcare provider about hepatitis C screening.
                </div>

                <h3>Risk Groups for HCV Testing</h3>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-position: inside;">
                    <li>People who use or have used intravenous drugs</li>
                    <li>Those who received blood transfusions before 1992</li>
                    <li>Healthcare and emergency workers</li>
                    <li>People with HIV infection</li>
                    <li>Those with a family history of hepatitis C</li>
                    <li>Incarcerated individuals</li>
                    <li>Individuals born during hepatitis C epidemic years (1945-1965)</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Testing Options</h3>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-position: inside;">
                    <li><strong>Antibody Test:</strong> Initial screening for past or current infection</li>
                    <li><strong>RNA Test:</strong> Confirms active infection</li>
                    <li><strong>Genotyping:</strong> Determines HCV strain for treatment planning</li>
                    <li><strong>Liver Function Tests:</strong> Assesses liver health</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- Call to Action -->
            <section>
                <h2>What You Can Do</h2>
                <p>If you suspect you might have hepatitis C or belong to a risk group, take action today:</p>

                <div style="background: #e3f2fd; padding: 25px; border-radius: 5px; margin: 20px 0;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1565c0; margin-top: 0;">Take These Steps</h3>
                    <ol style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li>Talk to your healthcare provider about hepatitis C testing</li>
                        <li>Get tested if you have risk factors</li>
                        <li>If diagnosed, seek specialist consultation</li>
                        <li>Discuss treatment options available to you</li>
                        <li>Begin treatment early for best outcomes</li>
                        <li>Attend follow-up appointments to confirm cure</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>

                <button class="cta-button">Learn More About HCV Treatment</button>
            </section>

            <!-- Key Takeaways -->
            <section>
                <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
                <div class="highlights">
                    <ul>
                        <li>Hepatitis C affects millions globally but is highly treatable</li>
                        <li>Modern Direct-Acting Antivirals offer cure rates exceeding 95%</li>
                        <li>Early treatment prevents progression to cirrhosis and liver cancer</li>
                        <li>Treatment typically takes 8-12 weeks with minimal side effects</li>
                        <li>Screening is crucial for people at risk</li>
                        <li>Barriers exist but solutions are available through public health initiatives</li>
                        <li>Seeking treatment benefits both individuals and public health</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>

